WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.476 --> 00:00:21.900
For a long time, Marius had a fever, which made him delirious and call out Cosette's name.

2
00:00:22.601 --> 00:00:27.565
Every day a very well-dressed gentleman with white hair came to inquire after the wounded man.

3
00:00:28.987 --> 00:00:33.297
At last in September, the doctor declared Marius out of danger.

4
00:00:34.017 --> 00:00:38.739
It had been three months since he was carried from the barricade to his grandfather's house.

5
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.921
Marius still had two more months of recovery ahead of him.

6
00:00:43.701 --> 00:00:49.784
Monsieur Gillenormand, relieved that Marius was alive, watched over him with great tenderness.

7
00:00:50.884 --> 00:00:55.746
As for Marius, he had one obsession: to find and marry Cosette.

8
00:00:56.866 --> 00:01:00.308
He had not said her name aloud since he recovered from his fever.

9
00:01:00.896 --> 00:01:05.259
He was not won over by his grandfather's constant concern and attention.

10
00:01:05.279 --> 00:01:08.862
"He's being nice to me now," thought Marius.

11
00:01:08.882 --> 00:01:13.405
"But as soon as I get well and mention Cosette, his old self will return."

12
00:01:15.046 --> 00:01:20.470
Marius was sure that Monsieur Gillenormand would once more object to his desire to marry Cosette.

13
00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:23.132
So Marius prepared to do battle.

14
00:01:24.312 --> 00:01:29.096
One day Monsieur Gillenormand made a comment about the fish that Marius was eating.

15
00:01:30.136 --> 00:01:34.698
Marius, who was looking for an argument, clenched his fists in anger.

16
00:01:34.718 --> 00:01:37.900
"This reminds me of something I've been waiting to say.

17
00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:39.580
I want to marry Cosette!"

18
00:01:40.501 --> 00:01:42.222
Monsieur Gillenormand laughed.

19
00:01:43.562 --> 00:01:51.726
"I don't see the connection between fish and Cosette, but of course you will marry her."

20
00:01:53.026 --> 00:01:56.088
Stunned, Marius trembled with happiness.

21
00:01:57.368 --> 00:02:01.851
"I know you expected an argument from me," continued Gillenormand.

22
00:02:01.871 --> 00:02:06.915
"But while you've been recuperating, I've been investigating her.

23
00:02:07.515 --> 00:02:09.297
What a lovely young woman!

24
00:02:10.077 --> 00:02:12.139
I insist that you marry her!"

25
00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:18.563
Then Gillenormand embraced Marius, and they both wept with happiness.

26
00:02:19.624 --> 00:02:23.246
"Now that I'm almost well, Father, don't you think I could see her?"

27
00:02:23.266 --> 00:02:27.289
"Since you called me ‘Father,' you shall see her today!"

28
00:02:28.469 --> 00:02:32.472
Later that day Cosette arrived on the doorstep of Marius' room.

29
00:02:33.232 --> 00:02:39.595
Monsieur Gillenormand, Aunt Gillenormand, and several servants were gathered at Marius' bedside.

30
00:02:40.636 --> 00:02:43.698
"Angel!" cried Marius when he saw Cosette.

31
00:02:44.578 --> 00:02:50.181
Cosette wanted to throw herself into Marius' arms but didn't dare to do that in front of so

32
00:02:50.221 --> 00:02:50.881
many people.

33
00:02:51.802 --> 00:02:55.504
A well-dressed man with white hair had entered the room behind Cosette.

34
00:02:56.064 --> 00:02:57.464
He had a sad smile.

35
00:02:58.105 --> 00:03:03.166
This was Jean Valjean, or Monsieur Fauchelevent as he was still known.

36
00:03:04.266 --> 00:03:08.047
Under his arm he was carrying a rectangular package wrapped in paper.

37
00:03:08.067 --> 00:03:12.288
"Does this man bring books wherever he goes?"

38
00:03:12.889 --> 00:03:14.969
Aunt Gillenormand whispered to a servant.

39
00:03:14.989 --> 00:03:20.371
"I believe he's a scholar," said Monsieur Gillenormand, who had overheard her.

40
00:03:21.471 --> 00:03:22.991
Then he bowed to Valjean.

41
00:03:23.011 --> 00:03:32.328
"Monsieur Fauchelevent, on behalf of my grandson, Baron Marius Pontmercy, I have the honor to ask for the

42
00:03:32.388 --> 00:03:33.829
hand of mademoiselle."

43
00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:36.991
Valjean bowed in agreement.

44
00:03:38.132 --> 00:03:42.915
Gillenormand turned toward Marius and Cosette, and extended his hands in blessing.

45
00:03:43.915 --> 00:03:46.417
"You have permission to adore each other!

46
00:03:46.437 --> 00:03:51.620
Now, the rest of you, talk loudly so these two can have some privacy."

47
00:03:52.828 --> 00:03:56.951
Everyone in the room burst into applause and began to talk at once.

48
00:03:56.971 --> 00:03:59.132
"I can't believe it's you!"

49
00:03:59.673 --> 00:04:00.593
whispered Marius.

50
00:04:00.613 --> 00:04:03.415
"Why did you go away to fight?

51
00:04:04.076 --> 00:04:06.677
I was so worried about you," said Cosette.

52
00:04:06.697 --> 00:04:11.561
"When I heard that we were coming here, I dashed out of the house.

53
00:04:12.101 --> 00:04:13.622
What must your family think of me?

54
00:04:14.002 --> 00:04:15.263
My collar is wrinkled.

55
00:04:16.084 --> 00:04:22.348
We're still living at 7 Rue She babbled on and on, completely happy at being with Marius again.

56
00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:25.321
"What a lovely daughter you have!"

57
00:04:25.801 --> 00:04:27.422
Gillenormand said to Valjean.

58
00:04:27.442 --> 00:04:30.543
"She is like something out of a painting.

59
00:04:31.163 --> 00:04:36.205
It's a shame she'll have to live very frugally because I don't have much money to give them."

60
00:04:36.225 --> 00:04:39.787
"Cosette has 600,000 francs."

61
00:04:40.387 --> 00:04:45.169
These were the first words that Valjean had uttered, and the room went silent.

62
00:04:46.309 --> 00:04:50.371
He laid on the table the package that Aunt Gillenormand had mistaken for a book.

63
00:04:51.111 --> 00:04:54.521
When he removed the wrapper, he revealed a stack of bank notes.

64
00:04:55.801 --> 00:05:00.802
"Actually it's closer to 584,000 francs," said Valjean.

65
00:05:00.822 --> 00:05:05.604
"Some of the money was spent on her education and household expenses."

66
00:05:07.344 --> 00:05:12.305
As for Marius and Cosette, they were so busy looking at each other that they paid little attention

67
00:05:12.366 --> 00:05:13.246
to this incident.

68
00:05:14.646 --> 00:05:16.207
Where had this money come from?

69
00:05:16.947 --> 00:05:20.908
Valjean had made a lot of money from the factory he ran as Monsieur Madeleine.

70
00:05:22.032 --> 00:05:26.855
After the Champmathieu trial, he had withdrawn 600,000 francs from the bank.

71
00:05:27.576 --> 00:05:31.158
This was the money that he buried in the woods near the Thenardiers' inn.

72
00:05:32.039 --> 00:05:36.662
Whenever he needed money, he would disappear for a few days to dig up some cash.

73
00:05:37.222 --> 00:05:41.726
He had also buried his other treasure—the bishop's silver candlesticks.

74
00:05:42.886 --> 00:05:48.030
When he read of Javert's death in the newspaper, he put the candlesticks on his mantel.

75
00:05:49.051 --> 00:05:53.185
Preparations were made for the wedding, which was to take place in February.

76
00:05:53.705 --> 00:05:58.747
Valjean did not want anything in his own or Cosette's history to prevent the marriage.

77
00:06:00.308 --> 00:06:05.790
So he invented a family for her and persuaded the nuns at the convent to sign the necessary

78
00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:12.513
certificates. At any other time, Cosette would have been upset to learn that Valjean was not her real

79
00:06:12.573 --> 00:06:16.734
father. But her happiness with Marius made those thoughts disappear.

80
00:06:16.754 --> 00:06:20.616
"I will always call you ‘Father,'" she told him.

81
00:06:21.728 --> 00:06:24.890
Cosette and Valjean came to visit Marius every day.

82
00:06:25.551 --> 00:06:29.834
The young man was healing well and now determined to find Thenardier.

83
00:06:30.995 --> 00:06:37.239
Marius knew Thenardier was a villain, but he still thought kindly of the rogue for saving his father's

84
00:06:37.319 --> 00:06:44.804
life. Marius also wondered about someone else—the man who had rescued him and brought him home to his

85
00:06:44.844 --> 00:06:50.088
grandfather. Marius seemed to recall having seen Fauchelevent at the barricade.

86
00:06:50.676 --> 00:06:52.637
He wondered if this were possible.

87
00:06:52.657 --> 00:06:56.798
"Are you familiar with the Rue de la Chanvrerie?"

88
00:06:57.198 --> 00:06:58.818
Marius asked Valjean one day.

89
00:06:58.838 --> 00:07:03.600
"I don't know anything about that street," replied Valjean.

90
00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:07.180
"I must have been dreaming," Marius said to himself.

91
00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:11.242
"I must have seen someone else who looked like Monsieur Fauchelevent."

92
00:07:12.222 --> 00:07:16.643
Marius hired people to search for these two men, but they were unsuccessful.

93
00:07:17.923 --> 00:07:19.844
At last the wedding day came.

94
00:07:20.652 --> 00:07:26.316
Cosette wore a beautiful white dress and a veil held in place with a wreath of orange blossoms.

95
00:07:27.477 --> 00:07:31.640
Marius looked handsome, and his grandfather beamed with pride.

96
00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:35.742
Valjean stood off to the side, his arm in a sling.

97
00:07:36.943 --> 00:07:40.546
After the wedding the guests went to Monsieur Gillenormand's for a banquet.

98
00:07:40.566 --> 00:07:42.207
"Where is my father?"

99
00:07:42.687 --> 00:07:44.228
asked Cosette as they sat down.

100
00:07:44.248 --> 00:07:49.832
"He asked me to say that his arm was bothering him and he went home," said a servant.

101
00:07:51.344 --> 00:07:53.246
In fact Valjean's arm was fine.

102
00:07:53.866 --> 00:07:57.549
He had bandaged it to avoid signing any papers connected with the wedding.

103
00:07:58.189 --> 00:08:02.672
As a former convict, he was not allowed to sign any legal documents.

104
00:08:03.773 --> 00:08:06.595
At home Valjean walked into Cosette's room.

105
00:08:07.396 --> 00:08:10.138
It was bare except for a few pieces of furniture.

106
00:08:11.099 --> 00:08:16.082
Her belongings had been taken away to the Gillenormands' house, where the couple would live.

107
00:08:17.123 --> 00:08:21.601
In Valjean's own room, he opened the small suitcase that he had always kept.

108
00:08:22.481 --> 00:08:26.743
He took out the mourning clothes that he had given to Cosette at the Thenardiers' inn.

109
00:08:26.763 --> 00:08:32.565
"She had that enormous doll," he remembered as he laid the clothes on his bed.

110
00:08:33.305 --> 00:08:35.105
"She took my hand and laughed.

111
00:08:35.966 --> 00:08:38.306
She had nobody but me in the world."

112
00:08:39.467 --> 00:08:42.928
Jean Valjean put his head down and sobbed.
